00:01.399 --> 00:04.099
Welcome to Pydantic for LLM workflows.

00:04.500 --> 00:08.000
In this course, you'll learn how you can use the Pydantic Python package

00:08.300 --> 00:11.000
to get an LLM to give you structured output.

00:11.400 --> 00:15.599
And by that, I mean output that's formatted exactly the way you want it to be.

00:16.300 --> 00:20.054
So of course, In general, LLMs produce free form text.

00:20.054 --> 00:23.605
And that can be great if you're doing something like summarizing an article

00:23.605 --> 00:27.015
or maybe coming up with an idea for a new recipe.

00:27.015 --> 00:30.765
But if you're building an LLM into a larger software system

00:30.765 --> 00:33.610
where you have multiple components and

00:33.610 --> 00:37.011
you want to be able to pass the data you're getting from an LLM

00:37.011 --> 00:40.110
to the next component in the system in a predictable way,

00:40.110 --> 00:43.511
that's when pydantic and structured output can be a big help.

00:44.110 --> 00:47.511
So for example, imagine you're building a customer support application

00:47.511 --> 00:49.610
where the user can submit a request.

00:49.710 --> 00:53.137
Maybe something like Hey, I forgot my password. or a complaint like,

00:53.417 --> 00:55.356
I'm not happy with the product I bought.

00:55.557 --> 00:58.366
You could pass that customer query to an LLM

00:58.557 --> 01:01.947
and have it generate a structured response that might look like this,

01:02.076 --> 01:03.746
where you have the user's information,

01:03.976 --> 01:08.632
like their name and email, the text of their request, a priority and a category.

01:08.632 --> 01:14.102
And then you might record some other things, like whether or not this request is a complaint.

01:14.102 --> 01:16.512
And maybe some tags or keywords about the request.

01:16.512 --> 01:21.292
Then your system could take this structured response and decide what to do next.

01:21.292 --> 01:25.078
Like if it's an urgent issue, you might create a support ticket

01:25.078 --> 01:28.158
and route it to a human support agent who can follow up and help.

01:28.628 --> 01:31.938
Or for this simple request of I forgot my password,

01:32.347 --> 01:36.817
you might pass the structured response to another LLM agent that can call a tool,

01:37.168 --> 01:40.558
like a function that can look up an FAQ response that could help the user.

01:41.644 --> 01:44.974
So maybe in this case that would be an FAQ response with a password

01:44.974 --> 01:46.834
reset link and instructions.

01:46.834 --> 01:50.603
So whether it's this step of having an LLM create a structured response

01:50.603 --> 01:52.353
that becomes a Support Ticket with

01:52.353 --> 01:54.614
this exact set of fields and content,

01:54.614 --> 01:57.463
or having an LLM provide the parameters you need.

01:57.638 --> 01:59.838
to call a tool and look up an FAQ response.

02:00.289 --> 02:04.519
You have very precise expectations for what each of those LLM responses

02:04.789 --> 02:08.389
needs to look like and what kind of data it needs to contain.

02:08.778 --> 02:11.318
So in this course, you'll learn different methods

02:11.558 --> 02:14.444
for using Pydantic to ensure that an LLM

02:14.444 --> 02:16.794
is giving you exactly the data that you need.

02:16.794 --> 02:19.925
Or in other words, you'll learn to validate the data

02:19.925 --> 02:21.955
that you're getting in a response from an LLM.

02:22.164 --> 02:25.495
And along the way, you'll also be learning data validation skills

02:25.495 --> 02:28.224
that can help you with handling a wide variety of data

02:28.224 --> 02:31.251
in really any kind of software system. And that might include

02:31.251 --> 02:33.450
things like human input to the system

02:33.450 --> 02:35.850
or external APIs and data sources,

02:35.850 --> 02:38.050
or really any data that you're wanting to pass

02:38.050 --> 02:40.450
from one component to the next in your system.

02:40.751 --> 02:43.050
It turns out that pydantic has actually been around since

02:43.050 --> 02:45.251
well before LLMs were a thing.

02:45.251 --> 02:48.151
And it's one of the most popular data validation frameworks.

02:48.336 --> 02:50.945
works out there. In fact, Pydantic sees

02:50.945 --> 02:52.936
over 300 million downloads per month,

02:52.936 --> 02:56.235
making it not just one of the most popular data validation frameworks,

02:56.235 --> 02:59.295
but really one of the most popular Python packages, period.

02:59.586 --> 03:04.355
And that's because data validation is at the core of really any software application.

03:04.806 --> 03:09.002
And so, in the lessons that follow, you'll be learning how you can use Pydantic

03:09.002 --> 03:11.062
to get structured output from an LLM.

03:11.062 --> 03:13.942
And you'll also be building data validation skills

03:13.942 --> 03:18.671
that you can use in any software application where you want to pass data from one component to the next.

03:18.671 --> 03:21.342
So, let's go on to the next video to get started.